course: adult-life
lesson: 15
title: When parents separate
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sometimes parents are so unhappy together for so long that they decide to stop living as a couple; this is called separating, and later it may become a divorce, the legal end of a marriage.
explain: A separation ends the marriage, not the family: both parents still love their children, and children never cause a split and cannot undo it.
explain: Picture a kitchen in Toronto: Mum and Dad sit you down together, Dad says "Mum and I have decided to separate", and Mum hugs you tight and says "It's not your fault" — and it is true.
explain: Afterwards life has new shapes: a second home with a room for you, a colour-coded calendar on the fridge, Sunday handovers in a car park, two toothbrushes, and lawyers and courts sorting out the grown-up paperwork.
explain: Hurt grown-ups sometimes say unkind things about each other and regret it; you never have to choose a side or carry their words.
fact: "We have something to tell you" means important news, good or hard, and parents plan such talks so they can tell children carefully and together.
fact: Separating means parents stop living together as a couple; it may lead to a divorce, but not always straight away.
fact: Grown-ups separate after thinking about it for a long time, because they are very unhappy together and cannot fix it.
fact: Parents say "It's not your fault" because many children secretly think they caused the split, and it is true: children's behaviour does not end a marriage.
fact: After a separation one parent often moves to a new home and sets up a room for the children so they feel at home in both.
fact: A solicitor is a kind of lawyer who helps with legal things like a divorce; seeing one does not mean anyone is in trouble with the police.
fact: A divorce is the official legal end of a marriage, and "the divorce came through" means the court has finished it.
fact: A divorce can feel sad and also a relief, because a long, hard process is over.
fact: Custody, or "arrangements", means deciding where children live and when they see each parent; if parents cannot agree, a mediator (a trained helper who helps them agree) or a family court helps decide what is best for the children.
fact: A shared calendar with "Dad's weekend" and "Mum's weekend" shows which home a child is at on which days, so everyone can plan.
fact: A handover is when one parent brings the child to the other; meeting in a neutral place like a car park keeps it calm and quick.
fact: Children with two homes often have two toothbrushes, two sets of pyjamas and two bedrooms, so they do not carry everything back and forth.
fact: Saying goodbye to one parent can feel sad even when you are happy to see the other, and both feelings are fine.
fact: A parent who says something unkind about the other and then "Forget I said that" has slipped; children do not have to agree or pass it on.
exercise: Mum and Dad sit you down together: "We have something to tell you." What does it mean? | They have important news to tell you carefully | You are in very big trouble for something you did at school | They want you to tell them a story | Dinner is going to be late
exercise: Dad says "Mum and I have decided to separate." What does it mean? | They will stop living together as a couple | They will sit at different ends of the table from now on at dinner | They are going on separate holidays this year | They are getting married again
exercise: Is separating the same as divorce? | No, divorce is the legal end of the marriage, and may come later | Yes, the two words mean exactly the same thing in law | No, separating is only for parents who were never married to each other | Yes, but only in Scotland
exercise: Mum hugs you tight and says "It's not your fault." Why do parents say this? | Many children secretly think they caused the split | Because it is a rule written in the law | Because it was partly your fault, but they have decided to forgive you | Because they want you to stop asking questions
exercise: Can a child stop parents from separating by being extra good? | No, children do not cause separations and cannot undo them | Yes, if the child is extra good every day for a whole year | Yes, if the child cries a lot | No, only grandparents can stop it
exercise: Dad moves into a flat with a spare bedroom. Who is the bedroom for? | For you, so you feel at home there too | For a lodger who pays him rent every month | For his work things | For guests at Christmas only
exercise: Mum says on the phone "I've been to see a solicitor." What is a solicitor? | A lawyer who helps with legal things like a divorce | A police officer who arrests people who have broken the law | A doctor for sad feelings | A builder who divides houses in two
exercise: Mum says quietly "The divorce came through today." What does it mean? | The court has finished making the divorce official | A letter about a holiday has arrived in the post | The divorce has been cancelled | Dad is coming back to live at home with the family for good
exercise: Why can a finished divorce feel like a relief as well as sad? | A long, hard process is finally over | Because the parents will get married again | Because divorces always give people money | Because it means nobody has to work any more
exercise: What does "custody" mean? | Deciding where children live and when they see each parent | Putting someone in prison | Keeping the family's money safe in a bank | Choosing which school a child goes to and which clubs they join
exercise: Who helps decide the children's arrangements if parents cannot agree? | A mediator or a family court | The children's class teacher at school | The next-door neighbour, who knows them | The oldest child in the family
exercise: The fridge calendar has "Dad's weekend" and "Mum's weekend" in different colours. What is it for? | Showing which home you are at on which days | Showing whose turn it is to cook the dinner each night | Showing when the bins go out | Showing who wins a family competition
exercise: Why do some parents do the handover in a car park? | A neutral place keeps it calm and quick | Car parks are the only place where it is allowed | Car parks have free parking at the weekend | So the children can play on the trolleys
exercise: Why do children with two homes have two toothbrushes and two sets of pyjamas? | So they do not carry everything back and forth | Because one set is for good days and one is for bad days | Because the law says every home needs its own set | Because the toothbrushes are for two different children
exercise: You feel sad saying goodbye to Mum even though you're happy to see Dad. What is true? | Both feelings are fine at the same time | It means you love Mum more than Dad | It means you must not go to Dad's | It means something is wrong with you
exercise: Mum says something unkind about Dad, then "Forget I said that." What does it show? | She is hurt and slipped, and knows you should not be in the middle | She wants you to tell Dad straight away | It is true and you must agree with her | She wants you to choose to live with her and never go to Dad's again
exercise: Are both parents still your parents after a divorce? | Yes, a divorce ends a marriage, not a family | No, only the parent you live with most of the time | Only until you are ten years old | No, the court chooses one parent
test: Your friend says "my parents are splitting up 'cos I kept fighting with my brother". What is true? | No, children do not cause their parents to split up | Yes, fighting with brothers and sisters often causes a divorce | Yes, so she should stop and they'll stay together | No, it is because of her brother only
test: overheard: "the solicitor says we can sort the house and the kids' arrangements without going to court". What is being sorted? | Money, the home and the plan for the children after the split | A police case against one of the parents for something they did wrong | Who is to blame for the separation | A school holiday for the children
test: Your brother says "Mum saw a solicitor, so she's in trouble with the police". Put him right. | No, a solicitor helps with legal paperwork like a divorce | Yes, solicitors only see people who have broken the law | Yes, solicitors are a kind of police officer | No, a solicitor is a doctor for families
test: Aunt Lou on the phone: "Signed, sealed, it's final as of this morning, how's she holding up?" What has happened? | The divorce has become official | The family house has finally been sold | Someone has passed a driving test | A new wedding has been booked for May
test: Grown-ups in the kitchen: "she wants Christmas Eve, he wants Boxing Day, it's a bloody nightmare". What are they arguing about? | Which parent the children spend each part of Christmas with | Which shop to buy the presents from | What to cook for Christmas dinner this year, turkey or beef or something else | Which day the bins go out at Christmas
test: You have a big football match on Saturday. How can the fridge calendar help? | It shows which home you will be at, so that parent can take you | It shows the football score in advance | It shows which parent is better at football | It decides whether you are allowed to play
test: Sunday, 6pm, Asda car park. Dad hugs you, says "see you Friday, champ", and you get into Mum's car. What is this? | A handover from one parent to the other | A trip to do the weekly shop | A visit to a solicitor | A babysitter collecting you
test: Your cousin is sad because her favourite teddy is at Dad's and she's at Mum's this week. What is a good idea? | Keep a small bag of the most important things that travels with her | Buy a second teddy and throw away the first one | Never take the teddy anywhere again | Ask to move to Dad's for good
test: Mum mutters "your father's useless, always was... forget I said that, love." What can you do? | You don't have to agree or pass it on, and can ask her not to say it | Tell Dad exactly what she said at the next handover in the car park | Agree with her so she feels better | Decide that Dad really is useless
test: Your little sister cries "if we're really really good, Dad will come home". What is true? | Children cannot undo a separation, and being good or naughty didn't cause it | Yes, being good for a month will bring Dad home | Yes, but only if both of you are good | No, Dad will come home only if she cries more
test: Your friend at school says "you've got two bedrooms? lucky, I wish my parents split up". How could you answer honestly? | It can be okay, but it also means missing one parent a lot | Yes, it is only good and never hard | No, two bedrooms means you are in trouble | Yes, everyone should want their parents to split up and get two of everything
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the cause of a parental separation. | It is a decision of the parents, not caused by the conduct of the children | It is ordinarily occasioned by the misbehaviour of the children of the marriage | It may be reversed by improved conduct on the part of the children | It is determined in every instance by the grandparents
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a divorce? | The legal termination of a marriage, which does not terminate the family | The legal termination of the relationship between each parent and the child | A temporary separation lasting not more than one week | A criminal proceeding against one of the spouses
exam: Identify the principal function of a family court in respect of arrangements for children, given that the parents are unable to agree. | To determine arrangements in the best interests of the children | To determine which parent was to blame for the separation | To impose a fine upon both parents | To require the parents to remarry
exam: Ascertain the purpose of conducting the transfer of a child between parents at a neutral location. | To ensure that the handover is calm and expeditious | To permit the purchase of groceries | To comply with the requirements of the police | To prevent the child from seeing either parent
